DX = ("CAT", "XAT")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 001
ps("V02-C-001", "Health and medicine",
   "Open visiting in intensive care has mostly helped, but only where units prepare for it.", "Easy",
   "For much of the twentieth century, intensive care units kept families at a distance. Visiting was confined to short, fixed periods, on the reasoning that relatives would spread infection, tire patients and get in the way of urgent work. As more units have experimented with open visiting, these fears have largely failed to materialise: patients with family nearby tend to be calmer, and relatives who watch the care being given often understand and trust it better. Yet the case for openness is not unconditional. Nurses in units that opened their doors overnight without adding staff or space have described exhaustion from the constant presence of anxious onlookers, and some patients want rest more than company. The lesson is not that restriction was simply wrong, but that flexible visiting works when units plan for it rather than merely permit it.",
   "Relaxing intensive care visiting rules has mostly brought benefits, not the feared harms, but those benefits depend on units preparing for open visiting rather than merely allowing it.",
   [("Families who stay close by in intensive care units help patients remain calm, and by watching the treatment closely they come to understand and trust the staff far better.",
     "This keeps only the benefits listed in the third sentence and drops the qualification that follows 'Yet the case for openness is not unconditional', so it is too narrow."),
    ("The old restrictions on intensive care visiting were mistaken from the start, and units should now allow families unlimited access at every hour of the day.",
     "The paragraph says 'The lesson is not that restriction was simply wrong' and warns that opening doors overnight without preparation exhausted nurses, so this contradicts it."),
    ("Open visiting in intensive care exhausts nurses and disturbs patients, so the fears of infection and disruption behind the old restrictions have turned out to be justified.",
     "The paragraph says the old fears 'have largely failed to materialise'; the exhaustion it mentions arises only where units opened without adding staff or space, so this reverses its finding.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (the feared harms of open visiting 'have largely failed to materialise' and patients and relatives benefit) and then qualifies it: openness is 'not unconditional', and 'flexible visiting works when units plan for it rather than merely permit it'. The correct answer keeps both the finding and the condition.",
   "Choosing the option that lists the benefits and forgetting the qualification, or swinging to the claim that the old fears were right.",
   "V02C-icu-visiting-hours",
   "The finding and its qualification are both stated plainly in the last two sentences; wrong options are clearly one-sided.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 002
ps("V02-C-002", "Health and medicine",
   "Defensive medicine is sustained by professional culture, not only by fear of lawsuits.", "Medium",
   "Defensive medicine is usually described as a doctor's rational response to the threat of lawsuits: order the extra scan, refer to the specialist, and no court can say you overlooked anything. The description is accurate as far as it goes, but it locates the problem too narrowly. Many doctors who practise in places where litigation is rare still order tests whose results will not change what they do. What they fear is less a judge than a colleague's raised eyebrow, a patient's disappointment, or their own memory of the one case that went wrong. Reforming malpractice law may therefore reduce unnecessary testing less than its advocates expect. The habit is sustained by a professional culture in which doing more is felt as diligence and doing less as risk, and a culture is not dismantled by a statute.",
   "Excess testing owes much to a medical culture that equates doing more with diligence, not just to fear of lawsuits, so changing malpractice law alone will achieve less than hoped.",
   [("Because doctors order unnecessary tests chiefly to protect themselves in court, reforming malpractice law is the most direct and effective way to reduce defensive medicine.",
     "This is the usual description the paragraph says 'locates the problem too narrowly'; it also predicts that legal reform will 'reduce unnecessary testing less than its advocates expect'."),
    ("Doctors who order extra tests are acting irrationally, since such tests never alter treatment and serve only to calm their own anxiety about mistakes in past cases.",
     "The paragraph says 'Many doctors' order some tests that will not change what they do; it does not say all extra tests are useless or call doctors irrational, so 'never' overstates it."),
    ("Even in places where lawsuits are rare, many doctors still order tests and referrals whose results are unlikely to change the treatment they finally choose.",
     "This is the supporting observation in the third sentence; it omits the conclusion about professional culture and the limits of legal reform, so it is too narrow.")],
   "The paragraph grants that the lawsuit explanation is 'accurate as far as it goes' but argues it is too narrow: doctors fear colleagues, patients and their own memories, and 'doing more is felt as diligence'. Hence legal reform will achieve less than expected, because 'a culture is not dismantled by a statute'. The correct answer keeps both the wider cause and the consequence for reform.",
   "Picking the option that repeats the familiar lawsuit explanation, which the paragraph mentions first but then qualifies.",
   "V02C-defensive-medicine",
   "The opening states the conventional view at length; the reader must see that the paragraph widens it rather than endorses it, and one wrong option is only the evidence.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 003
ps("V02-C-003", "Health and medicine",
   "Objections to diagnosing grief overlook that a label can secure help for the minority whose mourning is stuck.", "Medium",
   "When a bereaved person is still unable to work or sleep months after a death, there is a growing tendency to describe the condition as a disorder and to offer treatment. Critics object that this turns an ordinary human experience into a pathology, and that sorrow proportionate to a loss is not something to be cured. The objection has force, but it assumes that the only alternative to a diagnosis is respect. In practice the alternative is often neglect: a person whose grief has hardened into years of isolation may receive nothing, because friends have stopped asking and no institution recognises the need. A label can be misused, and grief that is merely intense should not be hurried. Yet a name can also be what makes help available to the minority for whom mourning has stopped moving.",
   "Though merely intense grief should not be hurried into treatment, critics of diagnosis overlook that a label may secure help for those whose mourning has become stuck.",
   [("Calling prolonged grief a disorder wrongly turns a normal and proportionate response to loss into a pathology, and so the practice ought to be resisted by doctors.",
     "This is the critics' objection, which the paragraph grants 'has force' but then rebuts by pointing out that the real alternative to a diagnosis is 'often neglect'."),
    ("Every bereaved person who is still struggling months after a death should be diagnosed and treated, since friends and institutions otherwise abandon the grieving.",
     "The paragraph insists that 'grief that is merely intense should not be hurried' and confines the benefit of a label to 'the minority', so 'every bereaved person' contradicts it."),
    ("Diagnoses of grief are usually misused, yet they still matter because the friends of the bereaved gradually stop asking after them and leave them isolated.",
     "The paragraph says only that a label 'can be misused', not that it usually is, and it ties the value of a diagnosis to the minority whose mourning has stopped moving, which this option drops.")],
   "The paragraph reports an objection to diagnosing grief, concedes its force, and then argues that it wrongly assumes 'the only alternative to a diagnosis is respect'. Its conclusion is qualified both ways: intense grief 'should not be hurried', yet a name can make help available 'to the minority for whom mourning has stopped moving'. The correct answer keeps the concession, the counterpoint and the restricted scope.",
   "Taking the critics' view, stated early and sympathetically, for the author's conclusion.",
   "V02C-medicalising-grief",
   "A critique of a critique: the reported objection is stated persuasively, and one wrong option gets the conclusion half right while distorting degree and scope.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 004
ps("V02-C-004", "Health and medicine",
   "Consent fails mainly through conversation: doctors forget their words are technical and patients do not ask.", "Easy",
   "Patients often sign consent forms for procedures they do not understand, and the usual explanation is that the forms are badly written. That is part of it, but the deeper difficulty lies in conversation. Doctors learn a technical vocabulary so thoroughly that words like 'benign', 'negative' or 'unremarkable' stop sounding specialised to them, even though a patient may hear a negative result as bad news. Meanwhile patients, unwilling to appear slow or to waste a busy doctor's time, nod rather than ask. Each side leaves believing that information has been exchanged. Consent obtained in this way satisfies the paperwork while missing its purpose, which is that the person agreeing should know what they are agreeing to.",
   "Consent often fails less through bad forms than because doctors forget their words are technical and patients are reluctant to ask, so the gap goes unnoticed.",
   [("Medical words such as 'negative' and 'unremarkable' mean something quite different to patients than they do to the doctors who have long used them at work.",
     "This is one illustration from the third sentence; it leaves out the patients' silence and the conclusion that consent misses its purpose, so it is too narrow."),
    ("Consent forms should be rewritten in plain language and patients trained to question doctors, since only then can hospitals be sure that consent is truly informed.",
     "The paragraph proposes no remedy and treats the forms as only 'part of it'; the prescription and the word 'only' add what the paragraph does not say."),
    ("Patients sign consent forms they do not understand mainly because doctors deliberately use technical language to discourage questions and to save their own time.",
     "The paragraph says technical words 'stop sounding specialised' to doctors, an unnoticed habit, not a deliberate tactic; it is patients who worry about wasting the doctor's time.")],
   "The paragraph sets aside badly written forms as only 'part of it' and locates 'the deeper difficulty' in conversation: doctors no longer hear their vocabulary as technical, and patients 'nod rather than ask', so that 'Each side leaves believing that information has been exchanged'. The correct answer states this two-sided mechanism and its result.",
   "Choosing the vivid example of 'negative' results instead of the general mechanism it illustrates.",
   "V02C-medical-jargon-consent",
   "The central contrast is announced in the second sentence; the wrong options fail on plainly stated points.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 005
ps("V02-C-005", "Cities and geography",
   "The scarcity of public toilets quietly restricts the city for many residents.", "Easy",
   "Discussions of what makes a city welcoming tend to dwell on parks, transport and housing, and rarely mention public toilets. Yet their absence quietly shapes who can use the city at all. A healthy young commuter can manage a long day out on the strength of a visit to a cafe; an elderly person, a parent with small children, someone with a medical condition or a delivery rider on a twelve-hour shift often cannot. Such people learn to plan their routes around the few places they may use, or to stay at home. Toilets are expensive to build and to keep clean, and badly managed ones become a nuisance, so their scarcity is understandable. But a city that leaves this need to the goodwill of cafes has, without ever deciding to, made its streets less open to some of its residents than to others.",
   "Though costly to provide well, public toilets matter more than urban debates admit, because their scarcity quietly limits the city for many residents who cannot rely on cafes.",
   [("Elderly people, parents with young children and delivery riders on long shifts often plan their routes around the few toilets they can use, or else stay at home.",
     "This repeats the illustration in the fourth sentence but leaves out the point it supports, that scarcity makes the streets 'less open to some of its residents than to others'."),
    ("Every city should build free public toilets on every street, since leaving the need to cafes is a deliberate policy of excluding poorer residents from public space.",
     "The paragraph says the exclusion happens 'without ever deciding to' and calls the scarcity 'understandable'; a deliberate policy and a demand for toilets on every street both distort it."),
    ("Public toilets are seldom built because they are costly and soon become a nuisance, which shows that cities are right to leave this need to cafes.",
     "The paragraph grants the costs only to reject the conclusion: leaving the need to cafes has made the streets less open to some residents, so this reverses its point.")],
   "The paragraph argues that a neglected amenity shapes 'who can use the city at all': people who cannot rely on cafes plan around the few toilets or stay home. It concedes that toilets are costly and that scarcity is 'understandable', but concludes that leaving the need to cafes makes the city less open to some residents. The correct answer keeps the claim, who is affected and the concession.",
   "Choosing the list of affected people, which is accurate but is only the evidence.",
   "V02C-public-toilets",
   "The claim is stated in the second sentence and restated at the end; the wrong options fail on plainly worded points.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 006
ps("V02-C-006", "Cities and geography",
   "Regulating street vendors should begin from accommodation, not removal, while protecting pedestrians.", "Medium",
   "City authorities often treat street vendors as a nuisance to be cleared: they block pavements, avoid taxes and make a district look disorderly. Each charge contains some truth, but together they describe vendors only from the point of view of those who drive past them. For the office cleaner who needs a cheap meal at six in the morning, or the migrant who cannot yet afford a shopfront, the stall is part of how the city works. Vendors also keep streets watched and lit after shops close, a service no budget pays for. None of this means pavements should belong to whoever claims them first; pedestrians have rights too. It means that regulation which begins from the goal of removal will usually destroy more than it orders, whereas regulation that begins from accommodation can address congestion without abolishing a livelihood.",
   "Since vendors serve many residents and keep streets safer, cities should regulate them with the aim of accommodating rather than removing them, while protecting pedestrians.",
   [("Street vendors block pavements and avoid taxes, so although they serve poorer residents, city authorities are justified in clearing them from busy districts.",
     "The paragraph grants that each charge 'contains some truth' but argues that regulation aimed at removal 'will usually destroy more than it orders', so this reverses its conclusion."),
    ("Pavements should be left free for vendors to use as they wish, since the cheap food and safety they provide outweigh any inconvenience caused to pedestrians.",
     "The paragraph explicitly says 'None of this means pavements should belong to whoever claims them first; pedestrians have rights too', so this contradicts its qualification."),
    ("Street vendors provide cheap meals for early workers, a foothold for migrants, and an informal watch over streets that stay open after the shops have closed.",
     "This lists the supporting points from the middle sentences but omits the argument about how regulation should begin, so it is too narrow.")],
   "The paragraph reports the case against vendors, concedes that it 'contains some truth', shows what it leaves out (cheap meals, livelihoods, watched streets), limits its own claim with 'pedestrians have rights too', and concludes that regulation should begin 'from accommodation' rather than 'from the goal of removal'. The correct answer keeps the reason, the conclusion and the limit.",
   "Choosing the list of benefits, which is accurate but leaves out what the paragraph argues for.",
   "V02C-street-vendors",
   "Two wrong options each capture one side of a balanced argument; the correct answer must hold the conclusion and the concession together.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 007
ps("V02-C-007", "Cities and geography",
   "Rivers mostly joined communities historically; their role as frontiers is largely a later political choice.", "Difficult",
   "Atlases encourage us to see rivers as natural boundaries: a blue line on the map separates one province or country from the next, and it is tempting to assume the line was drawn there because the water divided people. For most of history the reverse was closer to the truth. Before railways, moving goods by water was far cheaper than moving them by land, so a river bound the communities along its banks into a single zone of trade, kinship and shared speech, while the hills on either side were the real obstacles. Rivers became frontiers largely when states, wanting lines that were easy to describe in treaties and to defend, chose them for that purpose. The division we read into the landscape is therefore often an administrative choice laid over a geography that once worked the other way. This is not to deny that some rivers, wide or violent, have always separated peoples.",
   "The habit of seeing rivers as natural dividers mostly reflects later choices by states, since rivers usually joined the communities along them, though some have genuinely kept peoples apart.",
   [("Rivers have never truly divided human communities, and borders drawn along them are wholly artificial lines imposed by states that ignored how settlement actually took shape.",
     "The last sentence concedes that 'some rivers, wide or violent, have always separated peoples', and the paragraph says the division is 'often', not always, an administrative choice, so 'never' and 'wholly' overstate it."),
    ("States drew borders along rivers because, long before railways, river valleys already separated distinct communities that differed in their trade, kinship and speech.",
     "This reverses the relation: the paragraph says a river bound riverside communities 'into a single zone of trade, kinship and shared speech' and that states chose rivers for ease of description and defence."),
    ("Before railways, carrying goods by water cost much less than carrying them overland, which is why hills rather than rivers were the main barriers to trade.",
     "This is accurate but is only the supporting reason in the third sentence; it says nothing about the paragraph's main claim that river frontiers are largely political choices.")],
   "The paragraph critiques the view that rivers are natural boundaries. Its claim is that 'the reverse was closer to the truth' because water transport joined riverside communities, that rivers 'became frontiers largely when states' chose them, and, as a qualification, that some rivers 'have always separated peoples'. The correct answer keeps the reversal, the political explanation and the concession; the closest wrong option drops the concession and turns 'largely' and 'often' into absolutes.",
   "Choosing the absolute version, which sounds like a bold restatement of the thesis but contradicts the final sentence.",
   "V02C-rivers-connect",
   "The correct answer and the strongest wrong option differ only in degree; the reader must weigh the hedges 'largely', 'often' and the closing concession.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 008
ps("V02-C-008", "Cities and geography",
   "For many shrinking cities, planning for a smaller population is costly but more honest than chasing growth.", "Medium",
   "Cities that lose population are usually discussed as patients awaiting a cure: planners draw up schemes to attract new industry, new residents and new investment, measuring success by whether the numbers start rising again. A different approach, tried in some declining industrial towns, accepts that the population may not return and asks how a smaller city can be a good one. Vacant blocks become parks or allotments, services are concentrated in the districts that remain lived in, and the city plans for the people it has rather than those it lost. The first approach keeps hope alive but often spends heavily on growth that never arrives; the second risks being read as surrender and can abandon residents of emptying neighbourhoods. Neither is costless, but only the second is honest about the likely future of many such places.",
   "Though it has costs, planning for a smaller but liveable city is more realistic for many declining cities than spending heavily on growth that may never come.",
   [("Declining cities should concentrate services in occupied districts and turn empty blocks into parks, since trying to attract new industry is always a waste of public money.",
     "The paragraph says growth schemes 'often' spend on growth that never arrives, not always, and it admits the second approach has costs; 'always a waste' overstates it."),
    ("Shrinking cities may either pursue renewed growth or adapt to a smaller population, and each of these two strategies has distinct advantages as well as distinct costs.",
     "This reports the comparison but drops the verdict in the last sentence that 'only the second is honest about the likely future of many such places'."),
    ("Accepting decline is a form of surrender that abandons residents of emptying districts, so declining cities should keep investing in bringing back residents and industry.",
     "The paragraph mentions that the second approach 'risks being read as surrender' as a cost, not as its conclusion, and it ends by favouring that approach, so this reverses it.")],
   "The paragraph compares two responses to population loss, gives each a cost ('Neither is costless'), and then judges that 'only the second is honest about the likely future of many such places'. The correct answer keeps the preference, the admitted costs and the scope ('many').",
   "Choosing the even-handed comparison and missing that the paragraph ends with a verdict.",
   "V02C-shrinking-cities",
   "A neutral wrong option describes the comparison accurately; it is wrong only because the paragraph takes a side in its final clause.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 009
ps("V02-C-009", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Inability excuses only when a duty was never within one's power, not when one destroyed one's own ability.", "Easy",
   "A familiar principle in ethics holds that 'ought' implies 'can': it makes no sense to say that someone ought to do what they are unable to do. Nobody blames a person for failing to rescue a drowning child from a river on the other side of the world. The principle seems obvious, but it needs care. A man who could have repaid a loan but carelessly made himself unable to, by spending the money he had set aside for it, is not excused by his present inability. What the principle rules out, properly understood, is an obligation that was never within a person's power to meet, not an obligation that a person has put beyond his own reach.",
   "Inability cancels a duty only when the person never had the power to meet it, not when the person has carelessly made the duty impossible.",
   [("Whenever people are unable to do what is asked of them, they cannot be blamed for failing, whatever the reason they now find themselves unable to act.",
     "The paragraph denies that inability always excuses: the man who spent the money set aside for his loan 'is not excused by his present inability'."),
    ("Nobody can reasonably be blamed for failing to rescue a drowning child whom they could not possibly have reached in time to save from the water.",
     "This restates the opening illustration only; it misses the refinement that makes up the rest of the paragraph, so it is too narrow."),
    ("The principle that duty requires ability is mistaken, since people are often rightly blamed for failing to do things that they were in fact unable to do.",
     "The paragraph keeps the principle and says it 'needs care'; it narrows what the principle rules out rather than calling it mistaken.")],
   "The paragraph states the principle, grants its obvious core, and then refines it with the example of the man who spent the money for his loan: what the principle rules out is an obligation 'never within a person's power to meet, not an obligation that a person has put beyond his own reach'. The correct answer states exactly this refinement.",
   "Choosing the unqualified version of the principle, which the paragraph sets up only to refine.",
   "V02C-ought-implies-can",
   "The refinement is stated outright in the last sentence; wrong options are the unrefined principle, the opening example and a reversal.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 010
ps("V02-C-010", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Hypocrisy undermines a critic's authority, not the truth of the criticism, and admission can restore standing.", "Difficult",
   "It is widely held that a hypocrite has no standing to blame: the smoker who lectures his son about cigarettes, we say, should keep quiet. There is something right in this. Blame from someone who excuses the same fault in himself rings false, and it invites the reply 'Look who is talking'. But the view goes wrong if it implies that the criticism itself is mistaken. The son's smoking is no healthier because his father smokes, and a fault does not become acceptable merely because the person naming it shares it. What hypocrisy damages is the critic's authority to demand change, not the truth of what he says, and even that damage can be repaired if the critic admits his own failing. A hypocrite who says 'I do it too, and I am wrong to' has not forfeited the right to speak.",
   "Hypocrisy weakens a critic's authority to demand change but not the correctness of the criticism, and a critic who admits sharing the fault can recover the standing to speak.",
   [("People who share a fault have no right to criticise it in others, because blame from anyone guilty of the same failing always rings false to those who hear it.",
     "This is the widely held view the paragraph qualifies; it ends by saying a hypocrite who admits the fault 'has not forfeited the right to speak'."),
    ("Since a criticism is no less true when the critic shares the fault, hypocrisy does nothing to weaken anyone's standing to blame other people for that fault.",
     "The paragraph grants 'There is something right in this' and says hypocrisy 'damages' the critic's authority to demand change, so 'does nothing to weaken' contradicts it."),
    ("A hypocrite's criticism may be correct but carries no weight, and since nothing can restore his authority, he should leave the task of blaming to others.",
     "The paragraph says the damage to authority 'can be repaired if the critic admits his own failing', so the claim that nothing can restore it is false.")],
   "The paragraph separates two things the common view runs together: the truth of a criticism, which hypocrisy leaves untouched ('The son's smoking is no healthier because his father smokes'), and the critic's 'authority to demand change', which hypocrisy damages. It then adds that this damage 'can be repaired' by admitting the fault. The correct answer holds all three parts; each wrong option gets one part wrong.",
   "Choosing the option that defends the criticism's truth and overlooking that the paragraph does concede a real loss of authority.",
   "V02C-hypocrisy-standing-to-blame",
   "Each wrong option is right about one of the paragraph's three claims and wrong about another; the reader must track the distinction between truth and authority and the closing repair.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 011
ps("V02-C-011", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Since many interests are not interests in experiences, death need not end them, so the dead can be harmed.", "Medium",
   "Can the dead be harmed? The sceptic replies that harm requires someone to suffer it, and the dead suffer nothing. Yet we do seem to wrong a person when, after her death, we break the promise we made to her, scatter the collection she spent her life assembling, or spread lies about her. One way to make sense of this is to notice that many of our interests are not interests in experiences. A woman who wants her life's work to survive wants something to be true of the world, not merely to feel that it is; that is why being deceived about it during her lifetime would also harm her. If interests of this kind can be set back without the person's knowledge, then death, which ends experience, need not end every interest.",
   "Because many interests concern how the world goes rather than what a person experiences, they can be set back after death, so the dead may genuinely be harmed.",
   [("Since harm needs someone who suffers it and the dead experience nothing, breaking promises made to them can wrong only the living people who remember them.",
     "This is the sceptic's reply from the second sentence, extended; the paragraph argues against it by distinguishing interests from experiences."),
    ("A person who wants her life's work to survive is harmed if she is deceived about its fate, even if she never discovers the deception during her life.",
     "This is the supporting case about deception during a lifetime; it omits the conclusion about death, so it is too narrow."),
    ("The dead can be harmed because some form of experience continues after death, which is why lies told about them seem to wrong them so deeply.",
     "The paragraph describes death as that 'which ends experience' and rests its case on interests that do not depend on experience, so this contradicts its reasoning.")],
   "The paragraph answers the sceptic by noting that 'many of our interests are not interests in experiences'; such interests can be set back 'without the person's knowledge', as the case of deception shows, so 'death, which ends experience, need not end every interest'. The correct answer gives the premise and the conclusion together.",
   "Choosing the deception example because it is the paragraph's most concrete step, and missing the conclusion it leads to.",
   "V02C-posthumous-harm",
   "The argument is abstract and runs through an analogy; one wrong option is the analogy without the conclusion.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 012
ps("V02-C-012", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Pity and compassion differ in stance, not warmth: only compassion treats the sufferer as an equal.", "Medium",
   "Pity and compassion are often treated as the same feeling, but they place the one who feels them differently. Pity looks down. It registers another's misfortune from a position of safety, and it often carries a quiet relief that the misfortune belongs to someone else; this is why people who are pitied so often resent it. Compassion stands alongside. It begins from the recognition that the other person's suffering is the kind of thing that could befall anyone, oneself included, and so it treats the sufferer as an equal rather than as an object of concern. The difference is not that one is warmer than the other, for pity can be intensely felt. It is that only compassion leaves the dignity of the sufferer intact.",
   "Pity and compassion differ less in intensity than in stance: pity regards the sufferer from above, while compassion sees an equal and so preserves the sufferer's dignity.",
   [("Compassion is a deeper and warmer feeling than pity, which is why people who are pitied resent it while those who receive compassion feel respected by it.",
     "The paragraph says 'The difference is not that one is warmer than the other, for pity can be intensely felt', so this misstates the basis of the contrast."),
    ("People who are pitied often resent it, because pity registers their misfortune from a position of safety and carries relief that the misfortune is not one's own.",
     "This describes pity alone and leaves out compassion and the contrast that is the paragraph's point, so it is too narrow."),
    ("Pity is a selfish emotion that ought to be suppressed entirely, since it involves relief at another's suffering and treats the sufferer as an object of concern.",
     "The paragraph says pity 'often' carries relief, not always, and it recommends nothing; calling it selfish and urging its suppression goes beyond the text.")],
   "The paragraph contrasts the two feelings by where they place the one who feels them: pity 'looks down', compassion 'stands alongside'. It then rules out the obvious alternative ('not that one is warmer than the other') and ends with the real difference: 'only compassion leaves the dignity of the sufferer intact'. The correct answer captures the stance contrast and the denial that it is about warmth.",
   "Picking the option that ranks compassion as warmer, which the paragraph explicitly denies.",
   "V02C-pity-versus-compassion",
   "The tempting wrong option states the contrast in terms of warmth, which the paragraph rules out in one clause near the end.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 013
ps("V02-C-013", "Psychology",
   "Impostor feelings are often a reasonable response to exclusionary settings, not only an individual fault.", "Medium",
   "Impostor feelings, the private conviction that one's success is undeserved and will soon be exposed, are commonly described as a disorder of self-perception to be corrected by building confidence. The description fits some cases. But it treats as a fault in the individual a response that is often a reasonable reading of the situation. A first-generation student surrounded by peers who seem to know unspoken rules, or the only woman in a department, may feel out of place because she has been placed in a setting that signals, in many small ways, that she does not belong. Confidence training alone asks her to stop noticing those signals. It would be wiser to treat persistent impostor feelings as information about environments as well as about the people in them.",
   "Impostor feelings are not only a distorted self-image but often a sensible response to settings signalling exclusion, so they also carry information about environments.",
   [("Impostor feelings are a distorted way of seeing oneself, and the best remedy is training that builds the confidence of the individuals who privately experience them.",
     "This is the common description the paragraph says fits only 'some cases'; it argues that confidence training 'alone' misses what the feelings reveal about environments."),
    ("Impostor feelings are never really about the individual but are created entirely by unwelcoming institutions, so confidence training is pointless and should be abandoned.",
     "The paragraph concedes that the individual description 'fits some cases' and asks for attention to environments 'as well as' people, so 'never' and 'entirely' overstate it."),
    ("First-generation students and women in departments dominated by men are especially likely to feel that their success is undeserved and will soon be exposed.",
     "These are illustrations, and the paragraph makes no claim about which groups are most likely to have such feelings; the option misses its point about remedies.")],
   "The paragraph reports the usual view (a disorder of self-perception, fixed by confidence), grants that it 'fits some cases', and then argues that the feelings are 'often a reasonable reading of the situation' in settings that signal non-belonging. It concludes that they should be treated as 'information about environments as well as about the people in them'. The correct answer keeps the reinterpretation, its hedges and the conclusion.",
   "Swinging from the individual view to the opposite extreme and ignoring 'fits some cases' and 'as well as'.",
   "V02C-impostor-feelings",
   "The correct answer and the extreme wrong option share the reinterpretation; they differ only in degree, which turns on two short qualifiers.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 014
ps("V02-C-014", "Psychology",
   "Actions are regretted more in the short run, inactions more over a lifetime.", "Difficult",
   "People asked about a recent decision usually regret what they did more than what they failed to do: the hasty purchase or the unwise remark stings more than the opportunity let pass. Asked to look back over their lives, however, many report the reverse; the courses not taken, the things left unsaid, come to dominate. One explanation is that the consequences of an action are bounded and visible, so they can be repaired, excused or absorbed into a story of learning, while the consequences of inaction remain open, an unlived alternative that imagination keeps improving. If so, the familiar finding that actions are regretted more is not false, but it describes only the short run; over time the balance appears to shift.",
   "Actions tend to be regretted more in the short term but inactions more over a lifetime, perhaps because unchosen paths stay open to imagination while the results of actions are settled.",
   [("People regret what they fail to do more than what they actually do, so the familiar claim that actions are regretted more reflects a mistake about human memory.",
     "The paragraph says the familiar finding 'is not false' but 'describes only the short run', so declaring it a mistake reverses the paragraph's qualification."),
    ("Because the results of actions can be repaired or excused, people regret their actions less as time passes and, in the end, cease to regret them at all.",
     "The paragraph says only that 'the balance appears to shift' towards inaction; it never says regret for actions disappears, and the option drops inaction entirely."),
    ("Short-term regret centres on actions and long-term regret on inactions, which shows that memory distorts the past and makes early judgements of our choices unreliable.",
     "The first half is right, but the paragraph explains the shift by the open-endedness of inaction, not by a failure of memory, and it says the short-run finding 'is not false', so it does not call early judgements unreliable.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (recent decisions: actions regretted more), sets beside it a reversal over a lifetime ('many report the reverse'), offers a tentative explanation ('One explanation is', 'If so'), and concludes that the familiar finding 'describes only the short run'. The correct answer keeps both time scales, the tentative cause and the hedged tone; the closest wrong option has the right pattern but attaches an explanation the paragraph does not give.",
   "Accepting the first half of an option and not checking the explanation it adds.",
   "V02C-regret-action-inaction",
   "Two wrong options have the correct short-run and long-run pattern in part; one is separated from the correct answer only by the cause it supplies.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 015
ps("V02-C-015", "Psychology",
   "Satisfaction depends on whom people compare themselves with; upward comparison can motivate but often breeds discontent.", "Easy",
   "People rarely judge their own situation in isolation; they compare it with that of others, and the others they choose are usually those slightly better off. Such upward comparison can motivate, since a colleague who is a little more skilled shows what is within reach. But when the comparison is constant and the gap seems fixed, it breeds dissatisfaction even among people who are doing well by any absolute measure. A worker whose salary rises may feel poorer if her neighbours' salaries rise faster. Contentment, on this account, depends less on what one has than on whom one measures oneself against.",
   "Since people measure themselves against those slightly better off, contentment depends on whom they compare with; such comparison can motivate but, when constant, breeds discontent.",
   [("A worker whose pay increases may still feel poorer than before if the neighbours living around her are receiving larger increases each year than her own.",
     "This is the illustration in the fourth sentence; it omits the general claim about comparison and its two effects, so it is too narrow."),
    ("Comparing oneself with others is always harmful, since it makes even prosperous people dissatisfied, so people should judge their lives by absolute standards of wealth alone.",
     "The paragraph says upward comparison 'can motivate', so 'always harmful' contradicts it, and it offers no advice about absolute standards."),
    ("People usually compare themselves with those who are worse off, which motivates them to work harder and keeps them content even when their own incomes fall.",
     "The paragraph says people compare with 'those slightly better off', so this reverses the direction of comparison and its effect.")],
   "The paragraph explains a phenomenon: people compare 'upward', which 'can motivate' but, when constant, 'breeds dissatisfaction even among people who are doing well'. Its conclusion is that contentment 'depends less on what one has than on whom one measures oneself against'. The correct answer keeps the mechanism, both effects and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the salary example, which is vivid but only illustrates the point.",
   "V02C-upward-social-comparison",
   "The point is stated in the first and last sentences; each wrong option is a detail, an absolute or a reversal.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 016
ps("V02-C-016", "Psychology",
   "Corrected analyses suggest a modest hot hand exists: the belief is exaggerated, not baseless.", "Medium",
   "For decades the belief that basketball players get a 'hot hand', becoming more likely to score after a run of successes, was a textbook example of people seeing patterns in randomness. Analyses of shooting records found that hits did not cluster more than chance would predict, and the belief was filed alongside other errors of intuition. Later work identified a subtle flaw in the way those records had been analysed: the method used was biased toward finding no streakiness. Corrected analyses suggest that a modest hot hand may exist after all. This does not vindicate the fans entirely, since the effect appears far smaller than players and spectators believe. The episode shows instead that a debunking can itself rest on an error, and that a belief may be exaggerated without being baseless.",
   "The hot hand, dismissed as an illusion, may exist in modest form once a flaw in earlier analyses is corrected, so the belief is exaggerated, not baseless.",
   [("Later research has shown that sports fans were right all along about the hot hand, and that the earlier studies dismissing it should now simply be disregarded.",
     "The paragraph says the correction 'does not vindicate the fans entirely', since the effect 'appears far smaller' than they believe, so 'right all along' overstates it."),
    ("People readily see patterns in random events, as belief in the basketball hot hand shows, since shooting records reveal no clustering of successful shots at all.",
     "This is the earlier view the paragraph reports and then revises: corrected analyses 'suggest that a modest hot hand may exist after all'."),
    ("Since debunkings can rest on errors, beliefs that experts dismiss as illusions usually turn out to be correct once the original evidence is analysed again.",
     "The paragraph draws a lesson from one episode, that a debunking 'can' rest on an error; 'usually turn out to be correct' generalises far beyond it.")],
   "The paragraph traces a finding and its revision: the hot hand was dismissed, a flaw in the method was found, and corrected analyses suggest 'a modest hot hand may exist after all'. It then qualifies the revision ('does not vindicate the fans entirely') and ends that the belief 'may be exaggerated without being baseless'. The correct answer keeps the revision and the qualification.",
   "Treating the revision as a full vindication, or stopping at the earlier debunking reported in the first two sentences.",
   "V02C-hot-hand-belief",
   "The paragraph moves through three positions; wrong options capture the first, an overstated second and an overgeneralised lesson.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 017
ps("V02-C-017", "Society and culture",
   "Haggling, which looks wasteful to outsiders, discovers prices and builds customer relationships.", "Easy",
   "To a visitor used to fixed prices, haggling in a bazaar can look like inefficiency, a waste of time over sums too small to matter. For the trader it does useful work. In a market without price tags, the opening figure lets the seller learn what a customer is willing to pay, so that the wealthy visitor and the local regular can be charged differently without either being told so. The exchange also builds a relationship: a buyer who returns and bargains well is recognised, and the next deal starts from a friendlier figure. What seems like friction is, in such markets, the means by which prices are discovered and loyalty is formed.",
   "Haggling, which outsiders see as wasteful, lets traders learn what each buyer will pay and build ties with returning customers, so it serves real purposes.",
   [("Haggling over small sums wastes the time of buyers and sellers alike, which is why most modern markets have replaced it with fixed and clearly labelled prices.",
     "This is the visitor's impression that the paragraph sets aside; the claim about modern markets replacing haggling is not made at all."),
    ("Bazaar traders use haggling mainly to cheat wealthy visitors, charging them far more than local customers while pretending that the price is the same for all.",
     "The paragraph notes that different buyers can be charged differently but treats this as price discovery, not cheating; the moral charge and 'mainly' are added."),
    ("A buyer who returns to the same trader and bargains well is recognised, so that the next deal between them begins from a friendlier opening price.",
     "This is one of the two functions the paragraph describes; it leaves out price discovery and the general point, so it is too narrow.")],
   "The paragraph contrasts the visitor's view of haggling as 'inefficiency' with the work it does for the trader: it discovers what each customer will pay and builds a relationship with returning buyers. The last sentence sums up: what 'seems like friction' is how 'prices are discovered and loyalty is formed'. The correct answer states both functions against the outsider's view.",
   "Choosing the relationship detail, which is only half of what the paragraph claims.",
   "V02C-haggling-in-bazaars",
   "The two functions are named in the final sentence; wrong options are the rejected view, a moral distortion and one half.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 018
ps("V02-C-018", "Society and culture",
   "Monetising a hobby suits some, but for many it destroys the freedom from obligation that made it valuable.", "Medium",
   "It has become common advice that anyone with a hobby should turn it into a side income: sell the pottery, charge for the baking, open a channel for the guitar playing. The advice is not foolish, and for some people a pastime becomes a satisfying second livelihood. But it rests on the assumption that an activity is improved by acquiring a market price, and for many people the opposite is true. A hobby is valuable partly because nothing depends on it; one can do it badly, stop for a month, or follow a whim without disappointing anyone. Once customers, ratings and deadlines arrive, the activity begins to resemble the work it once offered relief from. Some pursuits are worth keeping precisely because they earn nothing.",
   "Turning hobbies into income suits some people, but for many it destroys what made the hobby valuable, its freedom from obligation, by making it resemble ordinary work.",
   [("People should never sell what they make in their spare time, since earning money from any activity inevitably destroys the pleasure it once gave them.",
     "The paragraph grants that 'for some people a pastime becomes a satisfying second livelihood', so 'never' and 'inevitably' contradict it."),
    ("A hobby is a valuable source of extra income, and people who develop their pastimes into small businesses often find in them a satisfying second livelihood.",
     "This is the common advice and its best case, which the paragraph concedes for 'some people' before arguing that for many 'the opposite is true'."),
    ("Hobbies are valuable mainly because people can do them badly or drop them for a month, which is why they make poor businesses that seldom earn money.",
     "The paragraph says a hobby is valuable 'partly', not mainly, because nothing depends on it, and it says nothing about hobbies making poor businesses or earning little; that conclusion is added.")],
   "The paragraph reports common advice, concedes that it works for 'some people', and then challenges its assumption: a hobby is valuable 'partly because nothing depends on it', and once customers and deadlines arrive it 'begins to resemble the work it once offered relief from'. The correct answer keeps the concession, the scope 'many' and the reason.",
   "Taking the conceded best case, stated early, for the paragraph's view.",
   "V02C-monetising-hobbies",
   "A concession and a reported view precede the argument; one wrong option borrows the argument's premise and attaches an outside conclusion.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 019
ps("V02-C-019", "Society and culture",
   "Casual dress codes replace learnable written rules with unwritten ones that still sort people, less visibly.", "Difficult",
   "When offices relax their dress codes, the change is usually welcomed as a move towards equality: without suits and ties, the argument goes, the junior clerk and the senior partner look alike, and people are judged by their work rather than their clothes. The intention is sincere, but the result is often a code that has become harder, not easier, to read. A formal rule could be learned once and followed by anyone who could afford a suit; the new norms, which separate the right kind of casual from the wrong kind, are rarely written down and are absorbed mostly by those already at home in the social world that sets them. The newcomer who arrives in the wrong trainers has broken no stated rule and cannot easily find out which unstated one she has broken. Relaxing a code, in short, need not relax its power to sort people; it may only make that power less visible.",
   "Relaxed dress codes, though meant to equalise, often swap learnable written rules for unwritten ones that favour insiders, so clothing may sort people as before, only less visibly.",
   [("Abolishing suits and ties in offices removes the visible markers of rank, so that junior and senior staff look alike and are judged mainly on the quality of their work.",
     "This is the hope the paragraph reports in its first sentence; it goes on to argue that the result is 'often a code that has become harder, not easier, to read'."),
    ("Offices should return to formal dress codes, because written rules about clothing are fairer than casual norms and can be followed by anyone who wishes to.",
     "The paragraph prescribes nothing, and it limits the virtue of formal rules to those 'who could afford a suit', so 'anyone who wishes to' and the recommendation both go beyond it."),
    ("Casual dress codes are adopted insincerely by employers who wish to keep newcomers out of senior roles while appearing to treat all their staff as equals.",
     "The paragraph says 'The intention is sincere'; the sorting it describes is an unintended effect of unwritten norms, not a hidden aim.")],
   "The paragraph reports the egalitarian case for relaxed codes, grants that 'The intention is sincere', and argues that the result is often harder to read: formal rules could be 'learned once', while casual norms are unwritten and 'absorbed mostly by those already at home' in the world that sets them. Its conclusion is hedged: relaxing a code 'need not relax its power to sort people; it may only make that power less visible'. The correct answer keeps the sincere intention, the mechanism, the hedges and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the prescription to restore formal codes, which reads the comparison as a recommendation, or the insincerity reading, which ignores an explicit concession.",
   "V02C-casual-dress-codes",
   "Two wrong options are close inferences a reader might draw (go back to formal codes; the policy is a cover) and are ruled out only by a short concession and the limited claim made for formal rules.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 020
ps("V02-C-020", "Society and culture",
   "Urban anonymity costs loneliness but gives a freedom close-knit towns seldom allow.", "Medium",
   "Small towns are often praised for their closeness: everyone knows your name, and help is never far away. Large cities are faulted for the opposite, a crowd in which no one notices whether you thrive or fail. The contrast is fair as far as it goes, but it leaves out what anonymity gives. To be known by everyone is also to be watched by everyone, and for a young person whose ambitions, beliefs or way of life depart from the local norm, the town's closeness can feel like a cage. The indifference of the city is what allows such people to try out a new self without asking permission. Loneliness is a real cost of urban life, but it is the price of a freedom that intimate communities rarely offer.",
   "Urban anonymity, usually counted only as a cause of loneliness, also gives people who depart from local norms a freedom that close-knit towns seldom allow.",
   [("Large cities are lonely places where nobody notices anyone else, whereas small towns provide the closeness and ready help that people need in order to thrive.",
     "This is the familiar contrast the paragraph calls 'fair as far as it goes' before adding what it 'leaves out', the freedom anonymity gives."),
    ("Young people whose ambitions or beliefs differ from those of their home town often experience its closeness as a cage from which they long to escape.",
     "This is one step of the argument; it leaves out the city's side of the comparison and the weighing of loneliness against freedom, so it is too narrow."),
    ("Anonymity makes city life better than small town life in every respect, since the loneliness that people complain of in large cities is largely imagined.",
     "The paragraph calls loneliness 'a real cost of urban life', so calling it imagined, and the city better 'in every respect', contradicts it.")],
   "The paragraph accepts the usual contrast as 'fair as far as it goes', then adds what it omits: being known is also being watched, and the city's indifference lets nonconforming people 'try out a new self'. It ends by weighing the two: loneliness is 'a real cost' but 'the price of a freedom that intimate communities rarely offer'. The correct answer keeps the added point and the concession.",
   "Choosing the extreme option because it favours the city, which seems to be the paragraph's side, without noticing it denies the conceded cost.",
   "V02C-urban-anonymity",
   "The paragraph takes a side while conceding a cost; the extreme wrong option is on the right side but drops the concession.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 021
ps("V02-C-021", "History and politics",
   "Period labels are indispensable but carry their coiners' judgements, which unwary historians inherit.", "Medium",
   "Historians divide the past into periods, and the names they choose do more than organise it. To call a thousand years of European history the 'Middle Ages' is to describe them as an interval between two more important times, a long pause between the classical world and its supposed rebirth. The label was coined by people who wished to see themselves as reviving antiquity, and it carried their judgement into textbooks for centuries. Periods are indispensable; without them the past would be an undifferentiated stream. But a period name is never a neutral container. It embeds a view about what mattered, and the historian who uses it without noticing this inherits a verdict rather than reaching one.",
   "Period labels are needed to organise history, yet they carry their coiners' judgements, so historians who use them unthinkingly adopt verdicts they never examined.",
   [("Historians should abandon period labels such as the 'Middle Ages', since dividing the past into named periods inevitably distorts how students and scholars understand it.",
     "The paragraph says 'Periods are indispensable'; its complaint is against using names 'without noticing' what they embed, not against periods or names as such."),
    ("The term 'Middle Ages' was coined by people who saw themselves as reviving antiquity, and it presents a thousand years as a pause between two greater eras.",
     "This is the example the paragraph uses; it leaves out the general claim about all period names and what historians inherit, so it is too narrow."),
    ("Because history without periods would be an undifferentiated stream, the names that historians give to periods are neutral containers that help to organise the past.",
     "The paragraph says the reverse: 'a period name is never a neutral container', even though periods themselves are indispensable.")],
   "The paragraph uses the 'Middle Ages' to show that period names 'do more than organise' the past. It concedes that 'Periods are indispensable', but insists that a name 'embeds a view about what mattered', so a historian who uses it unawares 'inherits a verdict rather than reaching one'. The correct answer keeps the concession and the warning.",
   "Choosing the option that rejects period labels altogether, which ignores the concession that periods are indispensable.",
   "V02C-historical-periodisation",
   "The concession and the warning sit in adjacent sentences; one wrong option keeps the warning and drops the concession, another keeps the concession and drops the warning.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 022
ps("V02-C-022", "History and politics",
   "Famines are often failures of access to food by particular groups rather than overall shortages.", "Medium",
   "It seems obvious that famines happen when there is not enough food. Yet several major famines have occurred in regions where the total supply of food was not much lower than in normal years, and some while food was being exported. What collapsed was not the harvest but the ability of particular groups to obtain food: labourers whose wages fell, artisans whose customers vanished, herders whose animals lost value as grain prices rose. A famine, on this view, is less a shortage of food than a failure of access to it, concentrated on those whose means of buying or growing it have broken down. This does not mean harvests are irrelevant, for a poor harvest is often what sets the collapse in motion; it means that counting the grain is not enough to predict who will starve.",
   "Famines often stem less from an overall lack of food than from the collapse of particular groups' ability to obtain it, though a poor harvest may set that collapse off.",
   [("Harvest failures play no real part in causing famines, which are produced entirely by falling wages and the loss of customers among the poorest groups of people.",
     "The paragraph says 'This does not mean harvests are irrelevant', since a poor harvest 'is often what sets the collapse in motion', so 'no real part' and 'entirely' contradict it."),
    ("Famines occur when the total supply of food in a region falls below what its people need, so the size of the harvest is the best guide to who will starve.",
     "This is the 'obvious' view the paragraph challenges; it ends by saying 'counting the grain is not enough to predict who will starve'."),
    ("During famines, labourers, artisans and herders suffer most because their wages, customers and animals lose their value just as the price of grain is rising.",
     "These are the examples of collapsed access in the third sentence; the option omits the general reinterpretation and the place of harvests, so it is too narrow.")],
   "The paragraph challenges the view that famines are simply shortages: several have occurred without a large fall in total supply, and what failed was 'the ability of particular groups to obtain food'. It then qualifies the thesis: harvests are not 'irrelevant', since a poor one 'is often what sets the collapse in motion'. The correct answer keeps the reinterpretation and the qualification.",
   "Choosing the absolute version that dismisses harvests, which overlooks the final sentence.",
   "V02C-famine-access",
   "The thesis is clear, but the correct answer must be told apart from an extreme version by the closing qualification.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 023
ps("V02-C-023", "History and politics",
   "Factories replaced task-based work with clock discipline, so punctuality began as an imposed habit.", "Easy",
   "Before factories, most work was organised by the task rather than by the hour. A farmer milked when the cows needed milking, and a weaver at home worked hard before a delivery and slackly after it. The factory changed this because its machines were expensive and had to be kept running, which required many workers to be present at the same moment. Clocks, bells and fines for lateness taught people to treat time as something owned by the employer and measured in units. The punctuality now regarded as a personal virtue was, in large part, a discipline that industrial work had to impose.",
   "Clock-measured work replaced task-based rhythms because costly factory machines needed workers present together, so punctuality began largely as imposed discipline.",
   [("Before factories, workers were idle and unreliable, and only clocks, bells and fines for lateness finally taught them the value of working hard every day.",
     "The paragraph describes task-based work, in which people worked hard when the task required; it never calls earlier workers idle, and its point is about punctuality, not effort."),
    ("A farmer milked his cows when they needed milking, and a weaver at home laboured hard before a delivery and slackly after it had been made.",
     "This restates the examples of task-based work in the second sentence and leaves out the change and its cause, so it is too narrow."),
    ("Punctuality is a natural personal virtue, and factories prospered because they could attract and keep workers who already possessed that virtue in abundance.",
     "The paragraph says punctuality was 'in large part, a discipline that industrial work had to impose', which is the reverse of a virtue workers already had.")],
   "The paragraph contrasts task-based work with factory time, gives the cause (expensive machines needing many workers 'present at the same moment'), names the means (clocks, bells, fines), and concludes that punctuality was 'in large part' imposed. The correct answer gives the change, its cause and the hedged conclusion.",
   "Picking the examples of pre-industrial work, which are vivid but only set up the contrast.",
   "V02C-factory-clock-discipline",
   "The causal chain runs in order through short sentences; wrong options are a detail, a reversal and a moral distortion.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 024
ps("V02-C-024", "History and politics",
   "A tradition's recent origin corrects a belief about its history but does not make it a sham.", "Difficult",
   "Many customs presented as ancient turn out, on inspection, to be quite recent: a ceremony said to go back centuries may have been devised a few generations ago, often at a moment when a group felt its identity threatened. Debunkers enjoy pointing this out, as if exposing the recent origin of a tradition exposed it as a fraud. The inference is too quick. A tradition does not need to be old to do what traditions do, which is to bind people to one another and to a shared past, and a recent invention that people adopt wholeheartedly is no less real in its effects than one inherited unbroken. What the discovery should change is not our regard for the practice but our belief about its history; the claim to antiquity may be false while the attachment it supports is genuine.",
   "Showing that a supposedly ancient tradition is recent should correct beliefs about its history, but it does not make the tradition a sham, since the bonds it creates are real.",
   [("Traditions that claim ancient roots but were in fact devised recently are frauds, since their whole appeal depends on a false story about their origins in the distant past.",
     "This is the debunkers' inference, which the paragraph calls 'too quick': a recent tradition can bind people just as an inherited one does."),
    ("Because recent traditions bind people as effectively as ancient ones, the false claims of antiquity attached to them are harmless and need not be corrected.",
     "The paragraph says the discovery 'should change' our 'belief about its history', so it does want the false claim corrected; only our regard for the practice should stay."),
    ("Traditions are usually invented when a group's identity is threatened, which explains why such new customs are adopted more wholeheartedly by their members than inherited ones.",
     "The paragraph says such inventions 'often' come at such moments and that adopted inventions are 'no less real', not more wholehearted; the comparison is added.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (many 'ancient' customs are recent), rejects the debunkers' inference that recency means fraud ('The inference is too quick'), and draws a two-sided conclusion: the discovery should change 'not our regard for the practice but our belief about its history'. The correct answer keeps both halves; the hardest wrong option keeps the first half and drops the second.",
   "Choosing the option that defends recent traditions so fully that it excuses the false history, missing that the paragraph still wants the belief corrected.",
   "V02C-invented-traditions",
   "The key's two halves are each mirrored in a wrong option that keeps one and inverts the other; the reader must hold the closing distinction exactly.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 025
ps("V02-C-025", "History and politics",
   "The name 'Silk Road' misrepresents a shifting network of short relays as one planned route.", "Easy",
   "The phrase 'the Silk Road' suggests a single highway along which caravans carried silk from China to Rome. The reality was a shifting web of routes, by land and sea, along which most goods travelled only short distances before changing hands. Few merchants made the whole journey; a bale might pass through a dozen markets, each trader knowing only the next stage. Silk was one commodity among many, and ideas, technologies and diseases moved along the same paths. The single name is convenient, but it can make a loose and long-lived network sound like a planned route.",
   "The name 'Silk Road' can suggest one planned highway, but trade actually moved in short relays along a shifting web of land and sea routes, carrying many goods.",
   [("Few merchants ever travelled the whole distance between China and Rome, so a bale of goods might pass through a dozen markets before it reached its final buyer.",
     "This is a supporting detail from the third sentence; it omits the paragraph's point about the misleading name, so it is too narrow."),
    ("Silk was a minor commodity on the routes between China and Rome, which carried chiefly ideas, technologies and diseases rather than goods meant for sale.",
     "The paragraph says silk was 'one commodity among many' and that 'most goods travelled only short distances', so goods were central; this distorts it."),
    ("Historians should stop using the name 'Silk Road' altogether, since it is a myth with no connection to any trade that really took place along those routes.",
     "The paragraph calls the name 'convenient' and describes real trade along the routes; it neither calls the trade a myth nor asks for the name to be dropped.")],
   "The paragraph contrasts what the name 'suggests' (a single highway carrying silk) with what it calls 'The reality': a shifting web of routes, short-distance relays and many kinds of cargo. It ends that the 'convenient' name can make a network 'sound like a planned route'. The correct answer states the contrast and its elements.",
   "Choosing the detail about merchants, which supports the point but is not the point.",
   "V02C-silk-road-network",
   "The contrast between name and reality is announced in the first two sentences and restated at the end.",
   cognitive="Understanding")
